+
+The following independent observations support the verdict.
+
+- **Authored mutation provenance is bounded to the operation actually being evaluated.** In packages/domain/src/mutations.ts, the optional pure failure mapper surrounds the authored expression/template, mutation validation and patch operations. The caller still owns classification. Message creation, IDs/clocks, provider/model resolution and provider completion are outside that mapping. The mapper receives the original operation index and operation kind. Applying the operation patch is also caught with that operation's index, so a JSON Patch operation number cannot replace the authored mutation identity.
+- **The public mutation failure has the required shape and recovery behavior.** packages/engine/src/handlers/mutate.ts emits nonresumable MUTATION_ERROR with node identity, zero-based operationIndex, operationKind and the bounded code/message cause. The human message uses the one-based ordinal. The cause message is bounded to 1,000 UTF-16 code units; private runtime exceptions are not blindly adopted as authored causes. Partial mutation work remains local until the handler returns. A failed later operation publishes no node.finished patch, so earlier operations from the failed node are not committed.
+- **Repair and fallback preserve their original continuation semantics.** packages/engine/src/handlers/common.ts maps a repair prompt's authored template error and a failed target write back to the pending coerce operation. Both repaired and raw-fallback target writes carry the original operation index. Continuation starts at pending.opIndex + 1. A resolver or completion provider throwing even the same error class stays outside the authored boundary. The final ContextThread validation still occurs after the node result in the manager: an invalid final thread remains an internal node defect rather than being assigned an invented mutation operation.
+- **Exit catches the actual evaluator failure.** packages/engine/src/handlers/exit.ts catches ExpressionError around evaluateExpression and emits nonresumable EXPRESSION_ERROR. Thread construction and value conversion remain outside that catch. The existing evaluator depth guard remains in place; the regression test's deep expression crosses that guard, while the wide literal control succeeds. No generic handler exception has become an authored Exit failure.
+- **New internal publication trusts an engine-created value, not a public details field.** packages/engine/src/failure-publication.ts introduces the internal RunFailureError subclass used by engine publication paths. It is not exported from the engine package's public index. Generic and untrusted INTERNAL_ERROR values reach failRun with safe text and without arbitrary exception details. Deliberate invariant summaries remain controlled engine strings. The raw error name/message/stack is retained in the private logger path with the available run/node identity; inference recording adds turn and operation correlation.
+- **The row and event share the safe failure.** packages/engine/src/run-manager.ts retains the existing terminal-state short circuit, script classification and bounding before appending run.failed and updating the run record. The same bounded failure is used for both. The changes do not add a new asynchronous operation to the mutation mapping, alter event sequence ownership, or replace the existing finalization/recovery locks. Context-thread and route invariant failures are safe, nonresumable engine defects.
+- **Cancellation and external-effect precedence are retained.** I traced the node catch ordering, child cancellation, termination safety, forced failure, deadline and abort handling, as well as classifyScriptFailure and the replay/resume policy. Script failure and uncertainty metadata retain their existing classification and resumability. Generic safe publication does not turn an uncertain submission into permission to submit again. Existing event-first crash recovery still reconstructs historical terminal failures as recorded.
+- **Secondary inference faults preserve evidence semantics.** Inference recording faults log raw diagnostics at capture and expose only the operation for harness.submission, turn.intent.reconcile and harness.status. The existing order and cardinality remain observable. Acceptance failure handling, before/after-commit reconciliation, cleanup, status recording, uncertainty and resumability retain their prior decisions. Non-internal provider contracts and their unrelated data are not globally scrubbed.
+- **Legacy child projection is specific and non-destructive.** The projection descends through the canonical childRunId / optional childNodeId / optional childDetails wrapper shape, validates its identities, and removes the recognized legacy internal exception fields or recording diagnostics. It does not recursively strip arbitrary provider objects that happen to contain name/message/stack keys. A newly emitted internal parent failure receives safe primary text; existing code, child/node identities, script disposition, recording order and recovery metadata survive. The old child rows and events are read, not rewritten. The subloop handler retains the child completion/failure ordering and its separate output-mapping handling.
+
+These conclusions come from the actual changes and surrounding implementation: contracts/run.ts; domain mutations, expression, template, pointer, patch and validation code; engine failure-publication, run-manager, recovery, errors, event-validation, decider-errors and script-dispatch; and the mutate, common, Exit, inference, subloop, script, heartbeat and decision call sites. The new contract value is reflected in both generated API representations. The domain callback keeps the existing dependency direction; there is no I/O introduced into the domain layer or new package boundary.
